From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brennoflavio <contato@brennoflavio.com.br>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 17:52:29 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] webapi: enforce a native script-element CSP subset

Honor script-src-elem -> script-src -> default-src in document CSP meta
policies and enforcing response headers. Apply parser meta elements on
insertion (void elements have no completion callback), ignore body/detached
meta elements, retain applied policies after mutation/removal, and combine
policies restrictively with first-directive precedence.

Check classic/module/import-map script elements at native preparation,
including dynamic insertion. Support none/unsafe-inline, nonce allowances,
SHA-256/384/512 inline hashes, self/scheme/HTTP(S) host sources, wildcard
subdomains/ports and literal paths. Preserve unpolicied behavior, DOM
contents and load bookkeeping. Avoid speculative fetches ahead of CSP
meta parsing; restrict explicit preload hints as well.

Check every script fetch redirect through a caller-owned guard that
survives submitSync's context replacement. Retain redirect targets across
classic/module preload adoption and recheck before evaluation, ignoring
source paths only on redirected requests. Match effective default ports
and HTTP-to-HTTPS upgrades.

Copy policy containers for about:blank/srcdoc/blob and initiating data:
navigations; queued navigation snapshots own their strings independently.
Keep Page.setBypassCSP state across document replacement but reset it on
target close. Add a native SecurityPolicyViolationEvent constructor,
readonly prototype accessors and asynchronous trusted violation events.
Update the existing CDP setup test for actual bypass state; add no tests.

This is a bounded Chrome-shaped script policy shim, not a security boundary
or full CSP3 implementation. It does not enforce eval/handler attributes,
module dependencies, workers, other resource directives, report-only
policies or reporting endpoints. Strict-dynamic trust propagation and
external integrity hash authorization are unsupported. Host/path matching
is lightweight rather than complete CSP URL canonicalization; violation
source locations remain unknown (empty/zero).

References:
https://www.w3.org/TR/CSP3/
https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#attr-meta-http-equiv-content-security-policy
https://chromedevtools.github.io/devtools-protocol/tot/Page/#method-setBypassCSP
